From: william-aqn <dark.crimson.d.c.r.m@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 12 Jul 2026 23:33:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] device: bound the buffer pool at 1024 (upstream
 iOS/Windows profile)

Prevents 'runtime: out of memory' on low-RAM routers under sustained inbound:
upstream's Linux profile leaves PreallocatedBuffersPerPool = 0 (unbounded), so
the receive path outgrows the heap faster than software ChaCha20 drains it.
Capping at 1024 (=64MB of 64KB buffers) turns WaitPool.Get into real backpressure;
excess UDP is dropped by the kernel as normal congestion signalling. Matches the
value this project previously applied as a CI sed; now carried as source so the
build tracks the fork.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 device/queueconstants_default.go | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/device/queueconstants_default.go b/device/queueconstants_default.go
index 80d3117..8087829 100644
--- a/device/queueconstants_default.go
+++ b/device/queueconstants_default.go
@@ -15,5 +15,5 @@ const (
 	QueueInboundSize           = 1024
 	QueueHandshakeSize         = 1024
 	MaxSegmentSize             = (1 << 16) - 1 // largest possible UDP datagram
-	PreallocatedBuffersPerPool = 0             // Disable and allow for infinite memory growth
+	PreallocatedBuffersPerPool = 1024          // bounded (upstream iOS/Windows profile): backpressure instead of OOM on low-RAM routers
 )
